about=f'''<section class="page-intro shell">{eyebrow('ABOUT / CHAITHANYA VEMURI')}<h1>Curious about<br><em>the whole system.</em></h1><p class="lead">I’m based in Berlin and studying Data Science, AI & Digital Business at Gisma University of Applied Sciences. My route here started in Electronics & Communication Engineering in Tirupati.</p></section><section class="shell story-layout"><div class="story-photo"><img src="/assets/chaithanya-vemuri.webp" alt="Portrait of Chaithanya Vemuri" width="460" height="460" loading="lazy"><span>Berlin, Germany</span></div><div class="prose"><h2>What kept my attention</h2><p>I started with Python and data analysis. Over time, I spent more of my effort on the work around the algorithm: turning event logs into tables, checking whether an experiment was fair, and getting an API to run consistently.</p><p>That is the kind of engineering I want to do professionally. My strongest work so far is independent project work and MSc research, and I’m looking for a junior role where I can contribute while learning how experienced teams build and maintain systems.</p><h2>How the projects changed my approach</h2><p>REES46 taught me to look at the operation that actually runs out of memory. Moving to monthly partitions was useful, but session lists still needed a different strategy. The second failure was more informative than the first.</p><p>My OTTO dissertation started with a Transformer. A weekly seasonal rule won the predefined development comparison. Keeping that result made the research more useful, even though it changed the story I expected to tell.</p><p>With FulfillAI, I wanted to follow the work beyond model evaluation. I built a small cloud delivery lab, deliberately introduced a bad image, and practised recovering the deployment. It gave me specific questions to bring to a real engineering team.</p><h2>Where I want to go next</h2><p>My main targets are junior Data Engineering and ML Engineering roles. I enjoy Python and SQL, recommendation and forecasting problems, and the infrastructure around model delivery. MLOps and platform work are a second direction I’m building toward.</p><p>I want to get better at working with other engineers: code reviews, shared ownership, changing requirements, and operating systems over time. Those are lessons a personal project can only partly teach.</p>{link('/background/','Education and training','button')}{link('/resume/','View my CVs','text-link')}</div></section>{contact_band()}'''
page('/about/','About','The path from Electronics & Communication Engineering to data pipelines, careful ML evaluation and systems work in Berlin.',about)

skills=f'''<section class="page-intro shell">{eyebrow('SKILLS / CONNECTED TO THE WORK')}<h1>A working toolkit.<br><em>With evidence.</em></h1><p class="lead">These are the tools I’ve used in independent projects and research. I group them by the engineering work they helped me do.</p></section><section class="shell skill-evidence">
<article id="data" class="skill-block reveal"><span class="skill-big-no">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">DATA FOUNDATIONS</p><h2>Make the data<br><em>usable first.</em></h2><p>Python, SQL, DuckDB, Parquet, PostgreSQL, dbt and pandas. PySpark / Redpanda for FulfillAI’s streaming implementation.</p><ul class="evidence-list"><li><strong>REES46:</strong> Bronze ingestion, canonical Silver tables, monthly Gold facts and hash-bucketed session aggregation.</li><li><strong>FulfillAI:</strong> synthetic relational data, SQL/dbt models, time-aware features and streaming.</li></ul>{ext(GH+'/rees46-v2/blob/main/docs/adr/001-partitioned-gold.md','Partitioning decision')}{ext(GH+'/fulfillai','Data platform source')}</div></article>
<article id="ml" class="skill-block reveal"><span class="skill-big-no">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">ML ENGINEERING & EVALUATION</p><h2>Give the baseline<br><em>a fair chance.</em></h2><p>scikit-learn, PyTorch, MLflow, recommendation retrieval/ranking, time-series evaluation, FastAPI and pytest.</p><ul class="evidence-list"><li><strong>REES46:</strong> chronological recommendation benchmark and local serving with an unknown-user fallback.</li><li><strong>OTTO:</strong> three development folds, fixed neural seeds, model comparison and a separate 96-hour holdout.</li><li><strong>FulfillAI:</strong> frozen thresholds and evaluation, including the WAPE / RMSE trade-off.</li></ul>{ext(GH+'/otto-demand-forecasting-dissertation/blob/main/docs/EXPERIMENT_DESIGN.md','Evaluation protocol')}{ext(GH+'/rees46-v2/blob/main/results/latest/model_benchmark.json','Ranking benchmark')}</div></article>
<article id="delivery" class="skill-block reveal"><span class="skill-big-no">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">DELIVERY & OPERATIONS LABS</p><h2>Understand what<br><em>happens after build.</em></h2><p>Docker, GCP/GKE, Terraform, GitHub Actions, OIDC / Workload Identity Federation, Helm, Argo CD, Prometheus, Grafana and Alertmanager. Linux and Bash support the lab workflow.</p><ul class="evidence-list"><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> keyless identity, image scanning, exact-digest promotion and GitOps reconciliation.</li><li><strong>Operations:</strong> metrics, alert routing, an intentional image-pull failure and a documented recovery exercise.</li><li><strong>Additional exercises:</strong> Jenkins, Ansible, ELK and OpenShift. These are narrower hands-on exercises.</li></ul>{ext(GH+'/fulfillai/blob/platform-engineering-v2/docs/cloudops/V2_OVERVIEW.md','Cloud lab evidence')}{ext(GH+'/fulfillai/blob/platform-engineering-v2/incidents/INC-002-bad-release.md','Recovery record')}<div class="editor-note">The cloud verification covers the API delivery, health and metrics path. It does not establish that the full data and ML stack ran in GKE.</div></div></article></section>{contact_band()}'''
